I'm working in the sagarmatha national  park in Nepal this National Park is  basically contains some of the largest  peaks in the world including Mount  Everest and I am looking at the impact  of tourism on the local livelihoods of  the communities of the Sherpa community  in the region in 1960s almost after a  decade when when this when Mount Everest  was first summited 20 people were the  first tourists who showed up in the  region and that's where that's how it  started in the current situation in  about 2008 there were about 30,000  tourists showing up in this region and  so tourism has progressed or increased  exponentially in the region and it leads  to a significant change in both the  culture the economy the resource  utilization and the chat the pressure on  the physical infrastructure of the  region the tourism is the least  considered to be the least destructive  activity in terms of resource  utilization in a region it has also seen  as a significant livelihood earner for  local community that it transfers a  significant amount of money in the  region but at the same time tourism has  these indirect effects on the region  that are not immediately seen but have  long-term impacts in the area and those  are the those are the issues that I am  grappling with one of the challenges  that tourism has brought in is the  pressure on the resources I would I  would like to say that because of its  incredible economic value people have  benefited significantly but by then by  the pure virtue of the size of the  tourism that is now in present in the  region the resources become really  stressed one of the resource for example  is the water resource and even though  these people  close to pressures the chances that  they're living very close to water is  low  they're usually villages are usually  established close to streams and some of  these streams have dried up so you can  often find in this region's water  pipelines that that run about a  kilometre from from the houses to the  nearest water source and with increased  number of tourists showing up there the  water scarcity is increasing in terms of  fuel this region has a more number of  people require more amount of energy and  that energy requirement it is fulfilled  by either using yak or cow dung or by  burning fuel wood extraction of fuel  wood then leads to degradation of local  forest resource while there have been  efforts made by a community and the park  management in the region to to reduce  that pressure or to manage it it's still  a challenge in terms of physical  infrastructure while the area now has  enough lodges and resource physical  resources to handle about twice the  amount of people that are coming right  now in terms of overall capacity the  challenge is that tourists show up in  this region and a very small window in a  year and so suddenly all the resources  get into extreme pressure for two or  three months and then then they are left  out and there's no use of them but  because of that sudden pressure there's  a physical infrastructure as heavily it  becomes vulnerable the solder master  national park management plan is ending  has ended by in 2012 the new management  plan requires greater method tourism  management understanding into it and my  research directly ties into providing  policy inputs into the sagarmatha  national park management plan  for the next five years I'm for that  reason I am working with the mountain  Institute and the mountain spirit who  were jointly engaged in this research  work and the the policy the the learning  of my research is going to be  disseminated to the local village  committees that have been actually  participating in the development of the  national plan and so the idea is to  empower and also provide a greater  understanding of these drivers of  tourism and create a discussion within  the community so that they can by  themselves come up with certain  understandings that can allow them to  manage what tourism means for them right  from the beginning in my research we  have engaged with local communities  there have been people who have been  directly involved in the research and  have an institutional memory of how we  have conducted the research over the  last three years in this we are trying  because of their continuous engagement  we have kind of locals who are involved  in understanding what are the challenges  that that we have touched on and how we  have come up with a solution so one part  of it is that these local people would  become our represent would become the  knowledge bearers of this research and  the second is that these flow diagrams  that we have developed the beauty of  these flow diagrams is that they don't  depend upon a particular language and  that means that this it is easily  transferable in terms of means of  symbols to to people who don't really  have a formal level of education and  hence for being still being a part of  research and understanding I was a 12  year old kid but when when my father  first sent me to the Indian side of of  Himalayas on a trekking camp and I was  mesmerized by that landscape and so  partly of course I am there's a little  bit of romanticize I mean me too to  follow the landscape but other than that  we have in terms of when I came to Yale  I realized that working in this region  really provides an ability to use the  training of the School of Forestry and  environmental studies of trying to link  and collaborate across these disciplines  and and issues like tourism which which  both have not only an economic impact or  a resource impact but also have cultural  impact they also have they can drive  communities in terms of migration all of  this can be studied in a very  interdisciplinary framework and this  topic becomes a really powerful one to  study it and that that's the most  exciting part of working in this region  where by a simple discipline one cannot  find a simple solution  you
 

